WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.752
Book Three, Proposition Twenty-Two.

2
00:00:02.752 --> 00:00:08.016
The opposite angles of quadrilaterals in circles are equal to two right angles.

3
00:00:08.016 --> 00:00:13.079
Let A B C D be a circle, and let A B C D be a quadrilateral inscribed in it.

4
00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:17.410
We are to prove that its opposite angles sum to two right angles.

5
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:22.040
Join the diagonals A C and B D by Postulate One.

6
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:35.845
By One Thirty-Two, in any triangle the three interior angles sum to two right angles; so in triangle A B C, angles C A B, A B C, and B C A together equal two right angles.

7
00:00:35.845 --> 00:00:49.074
By Three Twenty-One, angle C A B equals angle B D C, for both lie in the circle segment B A D C; and angle A C B equals angle A D B, for both lie in the segment A D C B.

8
00:00:49.074 --> 00:00:54.788
Therefore the whole angle A D C equals the sum of angles B A C and A C B.

9
00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.667
Add angle A B C to each.

10
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:04.730
Then angles A B C and A D C together equal angles A B C, B A C, and A C B—which equal two right angles.

11
00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:13.340
By the same reasoning, opposite angles B A D and D C B also sum to two right angles, as was required to prove.

12
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:25.622
Proposition Twenty-Two reveals how a circle constrains any inscribed four-sided polygon: its two diagonals partition one pair of opposite angles into four inscribed angles that reassemble inside a single triangle.

13
00:01:25.622 --> 00:01:37.652
As Heath and Todhunter emphasize, the converse is equally powerful: any quadrilateral whose opposite angles are supplementary is cyclic—a circle circumscribed about three of its vertices must pass through the fourth.

14
00:01:37.652 --> 00:01:49.960
